    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45792": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Actinide Solid State Science)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Actinide Solid State Science) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Actinide Solid State Science) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032142 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Dalton Nuclear Institute Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/09/2026 Contract Duration: 54 months School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description This Research Associate (RA) will form an integral part of the Actinides Centre of Excellence (ACE), which is a collaboration between the University of Manchester and AWE, and has the intention to grow by the inclusion of other national partners and universities. This role represents an exciting opportunity by helping to establish ACE (https://www.awe.co.uk/2026/06/awe-and-the-university-of-manchester-launch-actinides-centre-of-excellence/) - a national hub of collaborative research activity that aims to progress fundamental and low TRL Actinide Science & Innovation, intends to attract and train cohorts of future nuclear talent, and create tomorrow’s Subject Matter Experts. This role is part of a commitment from both the University of Manchester and AWE to advancing actinides research and innovation across thematic areas including materials recycle and solution process chemistry, and will play a pivotal part in shaping our future research landscape in nuclear science and engineering. The RA will be responsible for undertaking experimental research in actinide solid state science advancing the fundamental understanding of the behaviour of actinide materials. Research activities will include probing the performance of these materials under various relevant conditions as well as develop state-of-the-art characterisation techniques for actinide bearing materials with an emphasis on plutonium. This role provides the opportunity to work across a variety of specific project areas over the term of the post delivering high priority actinide research needs for AWE as determined by the ACE management team. A large breadth of actinide experimental research is likely to be undertaken across (but not limited to) the following: Physical, mechanical and electronic property measurements Microstructural analysis and imaging Materials signature studies Solid-gas corrosion measurements. The research associate will work with the ACE technical team to deliver commissioning of new glovebox facilities in the Nuclear Fuels Centre of Excellence dedicated to handling and studying plutonium materials. It is expected this research associate will closely engage with the other ACE research focus areas in materials recycle & solution process chemistry and analysis & spectroscopy, and contribute to the growth of ACE activities with the involvement of new partners and expanded research interests. This role, therefore, offers a significant opportunity to develop a wide range of high-level skills and expertise, providing a clear pathway to become a highly valued subject matter expert for the nuclear sector. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Robert Harrison Senior Lecturer Email: r.w.harrison@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032142 Research Associate (Actinide Solid State Science) FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
