      "title": "Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032141 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Dalton Nuclear Institute Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/09/2026 Contract Duration: start as soon as possible contract length dependent on start date, post funding expires 31/03/2031 School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description This Research Associate (RA) will form an integral part of the Actinides Centre of Excellence (ACE), which is a collaboration between the University of Manchester and AWE, and has the intention to grow by the inclusion of other national partners and universities. This role represents an exciting opportunity by helping to establish ACE (https://www.awe.co.uk/2026/06/awe-and-the-university-of-manchester-launch-actinides-centre-of-excellence/) - a national hub of collaborative research activity that aims to progress fundamental and low TRL Actinide Science & Innovation, intends to attract and train cohorts of future nuclear talent, and create tomorrow’s Subject Matter Experts. This role is part of a commitment from both the University of Manchester and AWE to advancing actinides research and innovation across thematic areas including materials recycle and solution process chemistry, and will play a pivotal part in shaping our future research landscape in nuclear science and engineering. The RA will be responsible for undertaking experimental research in actinide process chemistry and chemical engineering to assist in the development, optimization and sustainability of advanced actinide recovery processes. This role provides the opportunity to work across multiple projects over the term of the post delivering high priority actinide research needs for AWE as determined by the ACE management team. Experimental research activities are likely to vary from, for example, understanding actinide speciation in a wide range of solution process conditions to undertaking scale up experiments to support the design of advanced actinide recovery processes. Processes of interest may involve (but are not limited to): Dissolution Ion exchange/adsorption Solvent extraction, precipitation Electrodeposition operations; across primarily aqueous systems but could also involve organic and molten salt media. It is expected this research associate will closely engage with the other ACE research focus areas in analysis & spectroscopy and solid state science, and contribute to the growth of ACE activities with the involvement of new partners and expanded research interests. This role, therefore, offers a significant opportunity to develop a wide range of high-level skills and expertise providing a clear pathway to become a highly valued subject matter expert for the nuclear sector. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Clint Sharrad (Deputy Director of the Dalton Nuclear Institute) Email: clint.a.sharrad@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032141 Research Associate (Actinide Materials Recycle and Solution Process Chemistry) FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45422": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Apply for job Job reference: SAE-031817 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Earth & Environmental Sciences Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/09/2026 Contract Duration: 30 Months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an enthusiastic and talented Post-Doctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to join the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences at the University of Manchester and work with Filipa Cox and Joshua Lynn to explore plant-mycorrhizal fungal life-history strategies in relation to plant life-history. The project will address the following questions: 1) Do mycorrhizal traits match plant traits along the fast–slow life-history continuum? 2) Do plants and mycorrhizal fungi in symbiosis have complementary traits and, if so, is this driven by shared environmental conditions or by plant and/or fungal partner preferences? You will use a combination of macroecological and experimental techniques, including plant and mycorrhizal functional trait characterisation, molecular characterisation of fungal communities, working with “big data”, and characterising soil physicochemical properties. You will join a diverse, active and collaborative research group in the Soil and Ecosystem Ecology Lab at Manchester. You will be responsible for: Design and establish mesocosm experiments involving plants and mycorrhizal fungi Conduct and organise fieldwork to assess plant and fungal responses to environmental gradients Analyse and interpret resulting data and communicate results via presentations, and write papers for publication in leading journals Communicate research plans and findings with project collaborators and other staff working on the project Undertake knowledge exchange activities to generate wider impact of research We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Have a relevant PhD (or equivalent) in fungal, soil or plant ecology, and experience of field- and/or laboratory-based research Specialist knowledge on plant:soil interactions and fungal ecology Ability to travel to fieldsites in UK and Europe Experience in some/all research methodologies relevant to the post such as analysis of soil organic compounds, microbial community analyses, techniques to manipulate mycorrhizal fungal interactions and functioning, use of stable isotopes and enzyme assays Experience in analysis and interpretation of experimental data, including statistical analyses of large datasets, preferably from studies done at large spatial scales Desirable Criteria: Specific research experience in mycorrhizal fungal ecology Experience in the synthesis of plant-mycorrhizal symbioses in the laboratory Experience with species distribution modelling UK Driving licence We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Filipa Cox and Joshua Lynn Email Address: filipa.cox@manchester.ac.uk and joshua.lynn@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-031817 Research Associate in Plant-Mycorrhizal Interactions Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45922": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032265 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Research Associate with an interest in human plasma proteomics to join a pioneering project exploring the links between genetics and hypertension. This post creates a unique opportunity to generate high quality plasma proteomics data on a large patient cohort using mass spectrometry approaches. The postholder will then analyse these data, including appropriate quality control, and work with a wider team to integrate these data with additional relevant measurements, including genotypic and clinical data. The successful candidate will play an important role in developing and demonstrating the effectiveness of mass spectrometry-based proteomics at scale for human cohort studies, including the potential for large-scale multi-omics research for determining mechanisms underlying disease, such as protein-wide association studies. Excellent technical and organisational skills are essential, along with a passion for collaborative, data-driven research that contributes to improving people’s health. You will be responsible for: •    To take initiative in the planning of research and drive forward the progress of the project. •    To retain responsibility for the appropriate organization, storage and management of research samples, including Human Tissue Act-relevant material where appropriate. •    To plan and execute protein extraction and preparation protocols and liaise with colleagues for data generation. •    To conduct data analysis using a range of software and statistical tools. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: •    Hold (or expect to hold shortly) a PhD in a relevant area of research •    Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in mass spectrometry and/or protein quantitation •    Experience of mass spectrometry analysis of human-derived samples from large cohort studies, with proteomics experience strongly preferred. •    Experience in analysis and quality control assessment of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data Desirable Criteria: •    Experience in interpreting disease-related proteomics data, in particular processes related to age-related diseases or vascular health •    Experience in data analysis using DIA-NN •    Experience in pathway analysis and enrichment software, including but not limited to STRING, Reactome. •    Experience in establishing data analysis workflows and scripts in R. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: •    Generous employer contribution pension •    29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure •    Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Richard Unwin Email Address: r.unwin@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars with person specification linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032265 - Research Associate - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45916": {
      "title": "Research Project Coordinator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Project Coordinator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Project Coordinator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032261 Salary: £33,002 to £33,951 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/09/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months in first instance School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced, motivated and proactive Research Project Coordinator to work on the YouChoose Study. This role offers an exciting opportunity to work on a programme which will evaluate the NHS cervical screening programmes in England and Wales to determine if offering the choice of self- or clinician sampling is feasible, acceptable and improves user experience above the standard of care, effective at increasing screening participation, able to reduce health inequalities, effective for CIN2+ detection, and good value for money. YouChoose is sponsored by The University of Manchester. The post holder should have excellent communication and interpersonal skills. You should have some knowledge and an understanding of research governance processes within academic and/ or NHS settings and be able to manage busy workloads and prioritise to meet strict deadlines which will mean being flexible and responsive. The post holder will support the delivery of the portfolio of the research group, as directed by the Chief Investigator and Project Manager. Responsibilities will be varied and will include support with project management activities including data acquisition and management, ethics and regulatory approvals, quality control, reporting and document management in line with Research Governance standards. You will have experience of coordinating clinical trials within a clinical research environment, be enthusiastic and self-motivated, with good understanding of Good Clinical Practice and overarching regulatory guidelines. The post holder will be based within the Division of Cancer Sciences in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health at St Mary’s Hospital. You will be responsible for: Assist the Project Manager as required in maintaining the Trial Master File and monitoring aspects of the project Work closely with the eight NHS cervical screening laboratories to ensure confidential and efficient transfer of linked clinical data Work closely with the research team to ensure adherence to the protocol and regulatory guidelines Discuss and monitor risks to delivery, implementing changes to the project plans as directed by the Project Manager, ensuring that specific timelines are met and delivering against the programme’s objectives Liaising with colleagues across University departments, other universities, and NHS organisations, facilitating effective communication and engagement with investigators and collaborators About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Good (2.2 or above) biological science or social science degree or equivalent research experience Supporting and managing research projects or clinical trials preferably in an academic and/or NHS setting Study coordination of clinical trials including all aspects of research governance and delivery of research whilst working to set deadlines Excellent organisation, managing a varied workload Excellent oral and written communication skills, demonstrating ability to communicate effectively at all levels in a multi-disciplinary environment Desirable Criteria Experience of the University of Manchester’s electronic ordering system (iProc) or a similar system Trained in GCP and with a good understanding of the Research Governance Framework and ethical guidelines for work with humans and/ or human tissue Experience of organising and minuting meetings Producing progress reports to funding bodies, research ethics committees, senior managers etc. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chinenye Amadi Email Address: Chinenye.amadi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032261 Research Project Coordinator Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45892": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032237 Salary: £37,694  - £38,784 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/09/2026 Contract Duration: 17 Months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Job Description We are recruiting a postdoctoral researcher to take up a position within the Swamy group in the University of Manchester, to study potential new therapeutic targets for treating Coeliac disease. This is a fixed-term appointment for 1 year 7 months starting in September 2026. This is an exciting opportunity to be at the interface of clinical and basic research, and to collaborate with Dr. Hugo Penny and Prof. David Sanders at the National Centre for Refractory Coeliac Disease in Sheffield. The project will primarily involve studying human intestinal lymphocytes obtained from patient samples, and investigating the effects of different immunomodulators in controlling or exacerbating disease states. Patient samples will be obtained and processed in Sheffield, therefore the successful candidate must be willing to travel to and from Sheffield. The candidate will have experience in, or receive training in cutting-edge proteomics methods, and will have experience in analysing big data. A keen interest in deciphering the signalling pathways that allow autoimmune activation of T cells in Coeliac disease is a must. The successful candidate will have the chance to be involved in ground-breaking research that has high translational potential. There will be opportunities to present the research in national and international meetings and to publish the research findings. In addition, public engagement and patient involvement will form a key part of this position. There will also be opportunities to develop the research further with industrial partners, clinicians and other researchers. The successful candidate will be trained in cutting-edge proteomic technologies, human immunology, -omics analyses, spectral flow cytometry, and other state-of-the-art techniques. This is a unique opportunity to identify disease-relevant mechanisms using primary patient samples, and cutting-edge techniques, in a world-class research-intensive setting. Your priorities will include: Strong interest in studying disease mechanisms of Coeliac disease Must be willing to travel to Sheffield monthly Confidence in project implementation, decision-making, coordinating collaborative research Must be willing to work in a team and assist with training of PhD students and other lab members Keen interest in presenting scientific work at both national and international meetings, as well as to the public, and to talk to patients We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. Who we’re looking for: The successful candidate will: Have a PhD in Immunology, clinical research, or similar with strong academic record, and at least one publication/preprint demonstrating research output Have a strong interest in understanding the molecular basis of Coeliac disease Have a strong background in immunology, inflammatory bowel diseases, and mass spectrometry Have established skills with primary cell cultures and proteomics or other -omics analyses Have a strong ability to work independently but with the communication skills and openness to work in a team, and the willingness to foster an open and collaborative approach to science Have excellent communication skills and knowledge of the English language We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Mahima Swamy Email Address: mahima.swamy@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032237 Research Associate (Study the Molecular Basis of Coeliac Disease) (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45773": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology and Genomics) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032123 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Institute of Biotechnology Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: 6 months initially (exact contract length dependent on start date) School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This position forms part of Manchester’s development of synthetic biology approaches for next generation biotechnology, and will be based in Professor Patrick Cai’s ( yizhi.cai@manchester.ac.uk ) research group. The group is located in the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology (MIB), a unique cross-disciplinary research institute at the University of Manchester, which brings together more than 450 researchers with expertise in the foundational sciences supporting Synthetic Biology, including molecular biology, chemistry, engineering, materials science, computer science and medicine and as such is at the forefront of international developments in the emerging science of Synthetic Biology. The Cai laboratory has pioneered the design, construction and characterization of completely synthetic eukaryotic chromosomes. The group is actively exploring new applications of artificial chromosome technology, including the generation of novel natural product-like chemical matter. The Cai lab was recently awarded an ARIA grant to develop synthetic plant artificial chromosomes, and a Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation Global Challenge pilot grant to develop artificial chromosome-based strategies for discovery of novel anti-TB agents. We are now seeking to recruit a skilled Senior Technician to join the team with a good understanding and practical experience of modern molecular biology and biochemistry techniques. The purpose of this role is to: Provide expertise in lab media preparation, particularly specialised yeast media (e.g. SC, YPD, etc), and perform cell culture for bacteria, yeast and plant cells as required. Support and assist our ongoing research projects where required, in the area of synthetic biology and genomics, under the supervision of the PI. Work with the PI to ensure full compliance in microbiological health and safety procedures and be responsible for training / oversight of correct microbiological techniques. Assist the PI in scheduling management / coordination, as well as organising research group activities, including lab meetings and other events, lab inductions, and ensure safety systems and procedures are kept up to date and relevant. Oversee daily laboratory operations and manage the co-ordination of a growing and diverse research group to keep the research environment functional, compliant and well organised. Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Derren Heyes Email: Derren.heyes@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032123 Senior Technician (Synthetic Biology & Genomics) FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45407": {
      "title": "Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching & Research)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching &amp; Research) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health (Teaching & Research) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031774 Salary: £75,914 - £82,933 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Jean McFarlane Building, University of Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Division of Nursing, Midwifery & Social Work Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/09/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research, innovation and teaching and provide key leadership within the University and its partners, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will contribute to research, innovation and teaching across the organisation and provide key methodological leadership within the University. We are particularly seeking candidates who can demonstrate a strong research profile in community nursing and/or public health, in a relevant practice setting. Demonstrable impacts across digital healthcare, healthy aging, mental health, social research, long-term conditions or applied research methodologies, are particularly welcomed. You will be responsible for: Leading and contributing to the development of high-importance or interdisciplinary community nursing/public health research to achieve tangible benefits and impact for the School of Health Sciences Leading and sustaining research grant income and conduct research of national and international excellence funded by relevant external agencies Producing and disseminating high-quality, high-impact research outputs of national and international standing Providing cross-divisional PGR supervision and mentorship Providing academic leadership in your field, including the development, mentoring and career management of early and mid-career researchers, professional and research active colleagues We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in a relevant area Knowledge of community health nursing and/or public health nursing, research priorities and policy agendas A sustained record of success in obtaining research funding and delivering large-scale programmes and projects Evidence of leadership of research collaborations with national/international partners to advance global health or methodologies in the field A sustained record of successful doctoral supervision and assessments and PGR/PGT examinations Desirable Criteria: Current registration with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) or relevant professional organisation Specialist Qualifications in Community Nursing/Public Health Nursing We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Dawn Dowding, Head of Division Email Address: dawn.dowding@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Interviews for this post are expected to take place between 26th October and 6th November 2026. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-031774 Chair in Community Nursing/Public Health Further Particulars (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45893": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032245 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research into the electrical and optical characterisation of defects in high aluminium content AlGaN with a view to increasing the efficiency of UVC light emitting diodes within a dynamic and inclusive environment. The successful applicant will use advanced characterisation techniques to investigate defect behaviour and contribute to high-quality research outputs and international collaborations. You will be responsible for: - The electrical and optical characterisation of defects in high aluminium content AlGaN with a view to increasing the efficiency of UVC light emitting diodes. - Use Deep Level Transient Spectroscopy (DLTS) and photoluminescence measurements to characterise the impact of these defects on device performance. - Analyse the data produced by these experiments and contribute to the writing of journal papers based on the results. - Present work at academic conferences, both within the UK and overseas. - Travel to the Tyndall institute in Cork, Ireland, for collaboration meetings. - We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Please see the Further Particulars document linked below for the full person specification criteria. Desirable Criteria: Please see the Further Particulars document linked below for the full person specification criteria. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer contribution pension - 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure - Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof Matthew Halsall Email Address: matthew.halsall@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032245 - Research Associate in Semiconductor Device Physics - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45895": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Health Sciences Research",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Health Sciences Research Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Health Sciences Research Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032239 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Dentistry Hours Per Week: 17.5 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term until 31/05/2029 School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Health Sciences Research . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to applied health research activities across the Division of Dentistry within a dynamic and inclusive environment. The Division is home to Cochrane Oral Health, and a central aspect of the role will be supporting the delivery of high-quality systematic reviews that inform clinical practice, guideline development and health policy, helping to advance evidence-based oral health care. You will be responsible for: Strengthen evidence synthesis capacity within the Division through the conduct of high-quality systematic reviews. Support research impact, dissemination and stakeholder engagement activities, including through websites and social media. Support the development and submission of external funding applications. Contribute to teaching and assessment activities, as appropriate. Develop an independent academic trajectory, including applications for doctoral or postdoctoral fellowships. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold, or be nearing completion of, a PhD in a subject relevant to applied health sciences or a related discipline, or possess equivalent research experience. Experience of quantitative research and research methodology. Experience of conducting Cochrane and/or non-Cochrane systematic reviews. Experience of critical appraisal and meta-analysis. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with clinical and non-clinical collaborators. Desirable Criteria: Experience of network meta-analysis and/or meta-regression. Experience of stakeholder or public involvement in research. Experience of authoring peer-reviewed publications. Willingness to travel to national and international meetings. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Anne-Marie Glenny Email Address: warren.mittoo@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032239 - Research Associate in Health Sciences Research - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45510": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing Apply for job Job reference: HUM-031915 Salary: £42,254 - £71,566 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Management Sciences and Marketing Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/09/2026 Contract Duration: permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a T&R Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to actively to the research environment of the Marketing Group, the Division and the School, as well as contributing to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching across the programmes offered by the Division, in Manchester, online and, where appropriate, at our Global Centres, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. In your application, please indicate clearly whether you are applying for a Lecturer or a Senior Lecturer role. We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Emma Banister Email Address: Emma.Banister@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-031915 Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Marketing FPs1 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45810": {
      "title": "Divisional Administrator(Redeployees Only)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Divisional Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates Planned maintenance: This site will be unavailable on Friday 4th September between 18:00 and 21:00 (UK time). No action is needed, and any application you've already submitted will not be affected. List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Divisional Administrator (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032159 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/09/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated, organised and collaborative individual to join our Division of Pharmacy and Optometry as a Divisional Administrator. This role provides comprehensive and proactive PA, administrative, operational, people services and finance support to staff and activities across the Division. You will help ensure the smooth running of day-to-day divisional activity by coordinating meetings, events, recruitment and administrative processes, while working professionally with colleagues and stakeholders across the University. Please note that the earliest start date for this post is Monday 2nd November 2026 You will be responsible for: Providing proactive PA and administrative support, including diary coordination, meeting arrangements, agenda preparation, minute taking and following up agreed actions. Coordinating divisional meetings, committees, seminars, visitor programmes and events, ensuring logistics, materials and related arrangements are in place. Supporting recruitment and people-related administration, including selection arrangements, interview coordination, appointments, onboarding and induction activity. Supporting finance and procurement administration, including purchasing, expenses, invoice-related activity, payment forms and liaison with finance colleagues. Maintaining effective communication with academic, research, professional services and external stakeholders, signposting queries appropriately and escalating matters where required. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following essential criteria: Essential Criteria Experience of working in an administrative, customer-facing role and providing effective PA support, including diary management and meeting coordination. Experience of supporting meetings, committees or events, including arranging logistics, preparing documentation, taking minutes and following up actions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work professionally with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, meet deadlines, maintain accuracy and resolve routine problems effectively. Good IT skills, particularly in Microsoft 365 applications, with a proactive, flexible and collaborative approach to supporting colleagues and responding to changing priorities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Paula Lee Email Address: paula.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Divisional Administrator - G4 - JD BMH-032159 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/web-accessibility/": {
      "title": "Web accessibility statement",
      "description": "Web accessibility | The University of Manchester Skip to navigation | Skip to main content | Skip to footer Home Study Undergraduate Results and Confirmation Undergraduate Courses Prospectus (undergraduate) Applying (undergraduate) Offer-holders Fees and funding (undergraduate) Widening participation programmes Study experience (undergraduate) Student support (undergraduate) Contact us (undergraduate) Taught master's Taught master's Courses Admissions (taught master's) Fees and funding for taught master's study Why should I study a master’s? Teaching and learning (taught master's) Meet us (taught master's) Student support (taught master's) Why Manchester? (taught master's) Contact us (taught master's) Postgraduate research Why Manchester? (postgraduate research) Research areas Postgraduate research Programmes People (postgraduate research) Admissions (postgraduate research) Fees for postgraduate research Funding for postgraduate research Contact us (postgraduate research) Postgraduate certificates and diplomas Online and blended learning Continuing professional development courses Student terms and conditions, policies and procedures Student life Campus spaces Student experience City guide Open days Campus tours Accommodation Get ready for Manchester Virtual Manchester Making a payment International Why study at Manchester? (for international students) Accommodation for international students English education system International admissions Finance and scholarships for international students Study abroad and exchange Country-specific information Exhibitions and visits in your country Contact the international team Research Research highlights Advanced materials – research highlights Biotechnology – research highlights Cancer – research highlights Energy – research highlights Global inequalities – research highlights Research structure Research institutes Research platforms Research facilities Our Faculties Research strategy and achievements Research Explorer Researchers Publications Projects Research impact and innovation REF 2021 (research) Research impact showcase (research) SDG campaign (research) Research partnerships Postgraduate research Bicentenary PhD Studentships and Fellowships Researcher development Research environment Research vacancies Collaborate Business engagement and knowledge exchange Innovation Factory Policy Innovation Festival Make a donation Inspiring local communities Global influence About Our story Strategy to 2035 University structure People Key dates Rankings Our campus Maps and travel Open to the public (visitor attractions) Facilities Conference venues Our bicentenary year History and heritage Social responsibility Teaching and learning excellence Environmental sustainability News Events The University Magazine Governance Privacy information Connect Jobs Alumni Teachers and careers advisers Social media Contact us Give Close menu Share via Share by Email Share via Facebook Share via Facebook Messenger Share via Twitter Share via LinkedIn Share via Pinterest Share via Reddit Search Search text Alternatively, use our A–Z index Close menu Home Connect Jobs Search jobs Benefits of working here Disabled applicants International applicants Equality, diversity and inclusion Inclusive career opportunities Relocating to Manchester Application hints and tips Flexible working Home Connect Jobs Web accessibility Share Home Connect Jobs Search jobs Benefits of working here Disabled applicants International applicants Equality, diversity and inclusion Inclusive career opportunities Relocating to Manchester Application hints and tips Flexible working Web accessibility statement www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk The University of Manchester is committed to making its websites accessible, in accordance with the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018. This accessibility statement applies to The University of Manchester Jobs Website at www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk. Compliance status This website is partially compliant with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines version 2.2 AA standard, due to ‘the non-compliances’, ‘the exemptions’ or ‘the non-compliances and exemptions’ listed below. Non-accessible content The content listed below is non-accessible for the following reason(s): Non-compliance with the accessibility regulations WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard Not all functionality within the content is operable through a keyboard interface. Users will face accessibility barriers as keyboard-only navigation is unavailable, limiting access for individuals with motor disabilities who rely on keyboard input. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.1.1 (Keyboard). WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible Without a visible keyboard focus indicator, users who rely on keyboard navigation may struggle to know where they are on the page, leading to confusion and making the website difficult to use, especially for those with disabilities. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.4.7 (Focus Visible). WCAG 1.4.10 Reflow Content may require excessive scrolling. Users on small screens may struggle to access content, leading to frustration and lost information. Poor layout design can obscure important content, making the website difficult to navigate and diminishing the overall user experience. This fails WCAG success criterion 1.4.10 (Reflow). WCAG 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) When interactive elements like buttons or links are too small, users with motor impairments, such as those with tremors, arthritis, or limited dexterity, may find it difficult to accurately tap or click on them. This lack of precision can lead to mistakes, such as unintended clicks, which can frustrate users and hinder their ability to navigate the site efficiently. On touch devices, especially with smaller screens, the problem is magnified, making it harder for users to access important features or content, potentially excluding them from fully using the site. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name When the visible label of an interface element doesn’t match its programmatic label, users relying on assistive technologies may experience confusion. This inconsistency can make it harder to navigate and interact with the site, leading to frustration and preventing users from completing tasks or accessing important content. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.5.3 Label in Name. WCAG 1.4.3 Contrast (minimum) The visual presentation of text and images of text does not have a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1. Users may have trouble perceiving, understanding and interacting with content due to the poor contrast of content and navigation. This fails WCAG success criterion 1.4.3 (Contrast (Minimum)) WCAG 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context) The purpose of every link on the webpage isn't clear by reading the link text or by considering the text alongside its surrounding context. This means users don't know exactly where the link will take them or what it will do when clicked, creating confusion and frustration when unintended actions occur. This fails WCAG success criterion 2.4.4 Link purpose. WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value For all user interface components, the name and role cannot be programmatically determined; states, properties, and values that cannot be set by the user or programmatically set; and notification of changes to these items is not available to user agents, including assistive technologies. Users will have trouble understanding and interacting with elements as they lack proper labelling, role assignment, or value identification, leading to confusion and reduced accessibility, especially for users relying on assistive technologies. This fails WCAG success criterion 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). We are working with our supplier to remediate the above non-compliances and expect these to be complete Q4, 2024. Content is not within the scope of the accessibility regulations PDFs and other documents The accessibility regulations do not require us to fix PDFs or other documents published before 23 September 2018 if they’re not essential to providing our services. Any new PDFs or Word documents we publish will meet accessibility standards. Live video We do not plan to add captions to live video streams because live video is exempt from meeting the accessibility regulations What we’re doing to improve accessibility The University of Manchester is committed to improving digital inclusion and equity, ensuring that everyone has equitable access to information, technology, resources, and opportunities for learning and engagement, regardless of ability. We are undertaking a program of activity working with our Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion Division and Disability Advisory and Support Service to improve digital experiences for users with disabilities. Preparation of this accessibility statement This statement was prepared on 22/10/2024. It was last reviewed on 23/10/2024. This website was last tested on 14 October 2024. The test was carried out internally by The University of Manchester. The most relevant pages were tested using automated testing tools by The IT Services Division. Pages tested: Current jobs list Candidate login An individual job listing Apply for a job > I don't work here Feedback and contact information You can report accessibility issues and request support you encounter with this website using the following contact information. We aim to respond to your request in 3-5 days. Email: accessibility@manchester.ac.uk Enforcement procedure The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) is responsible for enforcing the Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018 (the ‘accessibility regulations’). If you’re not happy with how we respond to your complaint, contact the Equality Advisory and Support Service (EASS) . Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Contact details Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Road Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer Data Protection Copyright notice Web accessibility Freedom of information Charitable status Royal Charter Number: RC000797 Privacy preference centre Your privacy Strictly Necessary Cookies Performance Cookies Functional Cookies Targeting Cookies Your privacy When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. This information might be about you, your preferences, or your device, and is mostly used to make the site work as you expect. The information does not usually identify you directly, but it can give you a more personalized web experience. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. Click on the different category headings to learn more and change our default settings. Blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. More information Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. These cookies do not store any personally identifiable information. Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources, so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. All information these cookies collect is aggregated and therefore anonymous. If you do not allow these cookies, we will not know when you have visited our site. Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third-party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners. They may be used by those companies to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant adverts on other sites. They do not store directly personal information, but are based on uniquely identifying your browser and internet device. If you do not allow these cookies, you will experience less targeted advertising. Back Button Back Filter Button Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Confirm my choices Allow all"
    }
  },
